 ```
 
+### Strict Parsing
+
+Pass `{ strict: true }` to throw an error when a string cannot be parsed. By
+default, unparseable strings return `undefined`.
+
+```js
+ms('invalid')                   // undefined
+ms('invalid', { strict: false }) // undefined
+ms('invalid', { strict: true })  // throws Error: Cannot parse time string: "invalid"
+ms('2 days', { strict: true })   // 172800000
+```
+
+Strict parsing uses the same formats and 100-character limit as default parsing.
+The `strict` option does not affect numeric formatting and can be combined with
+`long`. Empty strings and invalid input types throw errors regardless of `strict`.
+
 ## Features
 
 - Works both in [Node.js](https://nodejs.org) and in the browser
